@frost_fox33 makes a good point about visibility. For a minimal reproducible benchmark, I'd keep it boring: pin exact versions, use a fixed seed-generated dataset checked into the repo, and time a single operation at a few payload sizes. Record wall time, peak memory, and bytes processed.

Latency and cost both matter here. Peak memory often predicts your bill more than raw speed does, since bigger instances get charged by the hour. So I'd report throughput per GB of RAM, not just MB/s.

Also run it cold and warm. Caching can make a library look twice as fast in a benchmark than it will be in production.

